Teach ANGLE's Vulkan backend to present to a HarmonyOS window.

The Vulkan path was never going to work here and the reason was not the
device. ANGLE picks its window-system layer by platform, and OHOS defines
__linux__, so it was taken for desktop Linux -- which looks for X11,
Wayland, GBM or VK_KHR_display and finds none of them. That is where "the
device's Vulkan is missing VK_KHR_display" came from; a phone has no more
business with VK_KHR_display than Android does.

What the device has is the Android-shaped answer: VK_OHOS_surface,
vkCreateSurfaceOHOS taking an OHNativeWindow, VK_OHOS_external_memory and
VK_OHOS_native_buffer, and a libvulkan.so where ANGLE's loader already
looks. So this is the Android backend with three names changed, plus the
platform macro that stops OHOS being mistaken for Linux -- placed ahead of
the linux arm, since both match.

None of the Android-specific machinery comes over: the blobcache
properties are Android's, and AHardwareBuffer siblings have no counterpart
here. Recordable configs are left out for the same reason -- HarmonyOS
records through its own path.

This gets --use-angle=vulkan as far as creating a display and a surface.
Dawn, Graphite and a Vulkan-backed WebGPU all sit on top of this layer and
are not touched; they come after it works.

Three things only a compiler knew, all of them from copying the Android
backend without building it:

EGLNativeWindowType is an integer on this platform, not a pointer, so
comparing it with nullptr does not compile. vkCreateSurfaceOHOS is not in
the Vulkan headers ANGLE builds against -- those are Khronos's and have
never heard of this platform -- although the loader on the device has the
symbol, so the entry point is fetched at runtime the way ANGLE reaches
any extension it was not compiled with. And GenerateConfigs has no
overload taking sample counts.

Taking OHOS out of ANGLE_PLATFORM_LINUX is what stops the Vulkan backend
looking for X11, and it also takes OHOS out of every other ladder that
only knew Linux. The GL backend was the first: no include, so no GL at
all. It gets plain EGL, which is what Linux without X11 uses, and desktop
GL says there is none rather than building a display that cannot work.


